1. [-**Keep a-]{+**Read the+} second set of books [-for one place.** For-]{+that already exists, and show where it stops.** Official environmental accounts already sit beside growth figures (see the first item in the list above), so the agent's job is to read them, not rebuild them. The first product is+} a [-town,-]{+line-by-line guide to one river basin's published water record: the Campaspe in Victoria, Australia, from 2003-04 to 2024-25.+}

{+   Victoria publishes water accounts with+} a {+page for each of its 29+} river [-basin-]{+basins in the 2021-22 and 2024-25 editions. Its archive holds editions from 2003-04 whose own description says they cover each basin, but no older edition has yet been opened to confirm this ([Victorian Water Accounts](https://accounts.water.vic.gov.au/); [Murray 2024-25](https://accounts.water.vic.gov.au/2025/local-water-reports/surface-water-by-river-basin/murray/); [previous accounts](https://accounts.water.vic.gov.au/water-explained/previous-accounts/); [Ovens 2021-22](https://accounts.water.vic.gov.au/2022/local-water-reports/surface-water-by-river-basin/ovens/)). The 2023-24 Campaspe page lists the environmental water holder's entitlements, for example 20,855 ML high-reliability and 4,394 ML low-reliability under the 2013 entitlement, and compares 2022-23 with 2023-24 ([Campaspe 2023-24](https://accounts.water.vic.gov.au/2024/local-water-reports/surface-water-by-river-basin/campaspe/)).+}

{+   The guide:+}
{+   - marks every change in layout, definition+} or {+unit. The accounts moved to+} a [-country, list-]{+new website and structure from 2021-22, with a mapping file for the old sections, so the older tables and units must not be assumed to match the newer ones ([about the accounts](https://accounts.water.vic.gov.au/about-us/); [East Gippsland 2023-24](https://accounts.water.vic.gov.au/2024/local-water-reports/surface-water-by-river-basin/east-gippsland/)). Otherwise a change of definition could be reported as a slow decline.+}
{+   - gives, for each line,+} what {+the line does not show, and the year it describes beside the year it was published. Otherwise a dry spell that+} is [-being run down, what-]{+already over can read as a present warning.+}
{+   - keeps a legal claim on water apart from water that actually reached the river. No line for water delivered to the environment has been confirmed. None+} is [-being built up, and what cannot-]{+to+} be [-rebuilt,-]{+inferred from rainfall, storage or flow, because an inferred figure set+} beside {+an official one looks official.+}
{+   - says plainly that volumes are not river health, and that saying nothing about soil, fish or wetlands is not a finding of no harm.+}
{+   - ends each section in questions for readers, not findings.+}

{+   Still to check before publishing: whether+} the [-GDP figure. Use-]{+agency's licence permits quoting its tables. Known limits:+} the [-accounts-]{+choice of basin and years is itself a selection, and a guide+} that [-already exist before making new ones.-]{+works where the books are unusually good does not show that the method works where they are thin. Thin books are where hidden harms are least visible.+}
2. **Watch for the doors that close for good.** Scan for moves under way that would cause loss that cannot be undone, and say so early, with the evidence. {+*Hold this step until the method has been tested blind on past years and the test published.* A false alarm about irreversible loss can harm a community's livelihood or an initiative's name. Water accounts cannot show what is irreversible on their own, and public records of such loss may not be current. The test runs as follows:+}
{+   - Before any later year is opened, publish a dated scoring file. It lists the chosen years (crossing the 2021-22 layout change), the early-year flags, and five outcomes: shown later, not shown, contradicted, could not be checked, and falls on a recorded layout change. The last counts as a likely false alarm.+}
{+   - Count false alarms beside hits, and list them first.+}
{+   - Score two simple benchmarks the same way: a lazy rule, such as flagging any line that falls two years running, and the basin's environmental water managers' own publicly stated priorities, copied word for word with their address. If the method does not beat both, it should say that it adds little.+}
